WEBVTT

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:04.050
During incidents, telemetry helps responders reconstruct events,

00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:07.695
identify affected systems and accounts, understand behavior, determine scope,

00:00:07.695 --> 00:00:10.160
and support containment and recovery decisions.

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:13.500
Evidence from multiple sources can establish what happened before, during,

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:14.440
and after an alert.

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:19.130
Responders often build timelines by correlating identity, endpoint, network, application,

00:00:19.130 --> 00:00:20.155
and cloud events.

00:00:20.155 --> 00:00:22.728
Consistent timestamps and identifiers are essential.

00:00:22.728 --> 00:00:25.950
A device name, account, IP address, session, request ID,

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:29.600
or cloud resource may connect records that would otherwise appear unrelated.

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:32.215
Retention and accessibility directly affect response.

00:00:32.215 --> 00:00:36.640
If important events have expired, were never enabled, or require a slow manual request,

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:39.310
teams may be unable to answer urgent questions.

00:00:39.310 --> 00:00:42.980
Incident planning should identify critical sources, access procedures,

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:46.420
evidence preservation needs, and fallback options before a crisis.

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:48.310
Investigations also improve telemetry.

00:00:48.310 --> 00:00:52.420
Lessons learned may reveal missing event types, unclear fields, collection failures,

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:54.750
or context that would have accelerated analysis.

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:58.700
Feeding those findings into engineering work makes future detection and response more

00:00:58.700 --> 00:00:59.995
efficient and defensible.

00:00:59.995 --> 00:01:03.080
Telemetry used as incident evidence should be handled carefully.

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:07.020
Access controls, integrity protections, documented exports, case references,

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:10.290
and consistent time handling help teams preserve confidence in the record.

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:14.295
Response procedures should identify who can retrieve sensitive sources and how evidence

00:01:14.295 --> 00:01:17.600
will be retained when normal platform retention is too short.
